    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:13:2",
      "id": "ex-signed-points-give-the-oriented-zero-bordism-invariant",
      "location": "[F3]",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The fact says: “classes of diffeomorphic oriented manifolds agree.” The cited thm-disjoint-union-makes-bordism-classes-abelian-groups, proof 1.1, concludes equality for “orientation-preserving diffeomorphic closed oriented manifolds.” A positive point and a negative point are diffeomorphic as smooth manifolds, but their signed counts are +1 and -1, so prop-zero-dimensional-bordism-groups gives distinct oriented bordism classes. The fact omits the essential orientation-preserving condition.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "3302d5f5d4578db0dd79d5a5abf3062b7dba0de19c519415c6594fa3410e48b2"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:13:3",
      "id": "ex-the-pair-of-pants-is-a-cobordism-realizing-addition-of-circles",
      "location": "1.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The Given defines C_+ as the circle centered at (-1,0), and 1.1 defines rho_+(x)=||x+(1,0)||^2-1/4. Nevertheless 1.1 asserts on C_± that “||x∓(1,0)||=1/2” and “||x±(1,0)||≥3/2”. Take x=(-3/2,0) in C_+. With the upper signs, the first displayed norm is 5/2, not 1/2, and the second is 1/2, not at least 3/2. The paired signs are reversed in these computations.",
